  "body_mappings": [
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        518.4614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        597.6614173228347
      ],
      "text": "On Monday 21 August 1911, when the Louvre was customarily closed, the Mona Lisa was discreetly plucked from its wall. The disappearance went unnoticed until the next day, when a painter arriving to sketch found a conspicuous gap of bare plaster. Custodians shuttered the museum for a week."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        430.7614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        509.9614173228347
      ],
      "text": "When the museum reopened, curious onlookers queued to stare at the vacant space where the masterpiece had hung. On 7 September 1911 the poet Guillaume Apollinaire was held in custody for about a week, and Pablo Picasso was interrogated. Both were cleared of involvement in the burglary."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        343.0614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        422.2614173228347
      ],
      "text": "The intruder was Vincenzo Peruggia, an Italian who had temporarily worked at the Louvre as a glazier. Having lurked in a storeroom on Sunday, he strode out unchallenged in a workman's smock, the painting tucked beneath his clothes. For more than two years it lay stashed in a false-bottomed trunk in his Paris lodgings."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        235.5614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        334.5614173228347
      ],
      "text": "In December 1913 Peruggia contacted Alfredo Geri, an art dealer in Florence. The Uffizi's director authenticated the painting at Peruggia's hotel; Peruggia was apprehended on 11 December 1913. Citing patriotism, he said he wanted it repatriated, mistakenly believing Napoleon had looted it. In fact Leonardo da Vinci had accompanied it to France at the invitation of King Francis I."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        128.0614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        227.0614173228347
      ],
      "text": "After showings in Italy, the painting was reinstated in the Louvre on 4 January 1914. In June 1914 a tribunal imposed a penalty of a year and 15 days, but he was imprisoned for about seven months. Many historians argue the heist elevated one esteemed gem among many to the world's most renowned painting. If so, its fame began with a bare wall."
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        537.6614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        597.6614173228347
      ],
      "text": "1911年8月21日是星期一，卢浮宫照例闭馆，《蒙娜丽莎》就在这一天被人从墙上小心低调地摘走了。画作的失踪一直无人察觉，直到第二天，一位前来画素描的画家发现，灰泥墙面上露出一块显眼的空缺。管理人员把博物馆关闭了一周。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        469.1614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        529.1614173228347
      ],
      "text": "博物馆重新开放后，好奇的围观者排队前来，盯着看/凝视那块空着的地方，那幅杰作原先就挂在那里。1911年9月7日，诗人纪尧姆·阿波利奈尔被拘押了约一周，巴勃罗·毕加索也受到审问。两人都洗清了牵涉这起盗窃案的嫌疑。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        380.6614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        460.6614173228347
      ],
      "text": "闯入者是文森佐·佩鲁贾，一个曾在卢浮宫短期做过玻璃工的意大利人。星期天潜伏在一间储藏室里之后，他穿着一件工作罩衫大步走了出去，畅通无阻（的）/未受阻拦（的），画就塞在他的衣服底下。此后两年多，这幅画一直藏匿在他巴黎住处一只带夹层底的大箱子里。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        272.1614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        372.1614173228347
      ],
      "text": "1913年12月，佩鲁贾联系了佛罗伦萨的艺术品商人阿尔弗雷多·杰里。乌菲齐美术馆馆长在佩鲁贾下榻的旅馆鉴定了这幅画；佩鲁贾于1913年12月11日被逮捕。以爱国为由，他说自己想把画送回祖国；（他）错误地以为拿破仑掠夺了/掠走了这幅画。事实上，列奥纳多·达·芬奇应国王弗朗索瓦一世的邀请，携/带着这幅画去了法国。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        183.66141732283472,
        461.8976377952756,
        263.6614173228347
      ],
      "text": "在意大利展出之后，这幅画于1914年1月4日在卢浮宫被放回原处。1914年6月，法庭判处了（佩鲁贾）一年零15天的刑罚，但他实际坐牢大约才七个月。许多史学家认为，这起盗窃大案把这幅众多画作中备受推崇的珍品抬高到了世界最著名的画作的地位。若真如此，它的名气（不过）始于一面光秃秃的墙。"
    }
  ],
